Mount contact-sheets as an engine

Gives FCPM the contact-sheets pipeline as a submodule at
`.contact-sheets-engine`, alongside `.advocate-engine`. The mount path is the
claim — `residency.yml` in that repository says so explicitly and declares
nothing about its own name.

## What it is

Byte-range frame extraction from Drive, assembled into contact sheets. It is
the pipeline `DESIGN-NOTES.md` already cites under *"Digitizing somebody's
tapes"*, and until today it had **no commits, no branches and no remote** —
most recently sitting as untracked files in a directory that was not itself a
repository. It is now `FC-Public-Media/contact-sheets`.

## The library wing is the mechanic

The engine holds no source of truth. Footage lives on Drive; everything it
writes locally is a residual — extracted frames, the thumbnail cache, the
assembled sheets, the publishable site. Measured before its first commit: a
3.5 GB working directory against a 0.18 MB repository, 1.7 GB of which was one
shoot's frames.

So it declares storage rather than carrying it. `residency.yml` names five
labels — `scratch`, `cache`, `sheets`, `index`, `publish` — each with `holds`,
`lifecycle` and `disposition`, and a host binds them somewhere with room and
may delete any of them between runs.

Two of those dispositions are load-bearing rather than decorative. `index`
holds the range and reconciliation documents, which **name people's files** —
the same reason the depot keeps its queue index in memory and never writes it
to disk. And the file closes by warning that no label may be bound to a studio
drive partition without checking, because those are under 4 GB and report
themselves as NTFS over Samba regardless of what they are.

## `update = none`, and the thing to decide

`publish-member-sites.yml` is the one workflow that checks out with
`submodules: recursive`. The engine is private, and a workflow's default
`GITHUB_TOKEN` is scoped to this repository alone, so it cannot read it.

`update = none` says the true thing — this engine is not an input to any build
here, so a checkout that skips it is correct rather than degraded — and it
should also stop the recursive checkout reaching for it. **That last part is
unverified against `actions/checkout`**; it is documented git behaviour and it
cannot make matters worse, but the Thursday run is what will actually prove it.

The durable fixes, neither taken here because both are Autumn's call:

1. **Scrub and publish.** `CLAUDE.md` records the Drive shortcut ID of the shoot
   it was written against; `folder-ids.conf` is already gitignored with an
   `.example` beside it. Take the first out and the repository can be public,
   and the submodule then works everywhere with no special casing.
2. **Give CI a PAT** that can read it, if it should stay private.

Until one of those happens the engine is a local mount, which is all it needs
to be — nothing in the site build touches it.
